      "response": {
        "category": "correctness",
        "consequence": "Running the Disqus import produces topics with wrong creation dates and no category placement. Existing scripts that pass `-c` lose their category with no warning, and the import can abort partway when a source page is not fetchable.",
        "evidence_refs": [
          "source:new:lib/tasks/disqus.thor@sha256:f1e3eae78c2e27f951d8626fc51de67bd82d75369092c145cf9fe75174f50253",
          "source:old:lib/tasks/disqus.thor@sha256:deb672af79eedceea360dd99e16c608b13d550af224d6ab4ce7f6a4048fb5d6d"
        ],
        "explanation": "The change removes the `--category` option and replaces PostCreator with `TopicEmbed.import_remote(user, t[:link], title: t[:title])`. That call accepts only a title. `t[:created_at]` is no longer passed, so imported topics are dated at import time instead of the Disqus thread date. The category lookup is gone, so every imported topic lands in the default category. `import_remote` also fetches `t[:link]` over HTTP, so an unreachable article URL raises mid-import. It also needs the new `ruby-readability` gem.",
        "lines": {
          "end_line": 148,
          "start_line": 148
        },
        "other_findings": [
          {
            "category": "persistence_migration",
            "consequence": "A re-run or a migration-version mismatch silently destroys the stored embed mappings.",
            "evidence_refs": [
              "source:new:db/migrate/20131217174004_create_topic_embeds.rb@sha256:d3838c0471d17c40f97792cd5bdf1a5f2ac0bab0e6509f202553eadc50dd65f9"
            ],
            "explanation": "The CreateTopicEmbeds migration uses `force: true`. If the migration runs against a database where `topic_embeds` already exists, it drops that table and all its rows before recreating it. The same pattern was added to the existing CreateTopTopics migration.",
            "lines": {
              "end_line": 3,
              "start_line": 3
            },
            "replacement": null,
